<p>This just made me go &quot;wat&quot;, especially given that:</p>
<p>- I'm a brony<br />
- I'm from Singapore<br />
- I'm a railfan</p>
<p>It's like this SCP speaks to me. lol.</p>
<p>Nitpick: in Singapore, except for internal purposes train platforms are referred to by letters. Also, each line's platforms has their own set of letters, except for platforms used by multiple lines in a cross-platform interchange transfer setup (City Hall, Raffles Place, Bayfront, and Jurong East stations as of now). So for example, at Dhoby Ghaut, the North-South Line platforms are lettered A and B, separately from the North-East Line platforms which are also A and B, and again separately from the Circle Line platforms which are also A and B. Confusing, I know.<br />
You might want to change it to &quot;Platform A&quot; or &quot;Platform B of the Circle Line&quot;.</p>
<p>Here's an idea: one of the terminus station on the Circle Line, HarbourFront, is also an interchange station with another line; on the Circle Line side there is a platform, designed to be used as an alighting-only terminating platform but, as far as I know, is never used in practice. The platform's partly cordoned off and staff will guide you to the &quot;right&quot; platform if you stray there. You might want to &quot;relocate&quot; the anomaly there if you want to; I can try supplying photos of that particular platform and its letter.</p>

